HOW REAL CASCADES PRODUCE THIS PATTERN
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

The cluster naturally produces this kind of request spam when:
- Multiple controllers restart simultaneously (each does full LIST on startup)
- Flux reconciles many kustomizations
- Prometheus scraping during controller churn
- "Watch" disconnects and reconnects (forces full resync LIST)
- Post-cascade recovery (everything restarting at once)

This is EXACTLY what happened tonight when master1 hung and came back.

Updated unified theory:
```
Trigger: anything that causes multiple controllers to restart
         simultaneously (OOM, qemu-ga spike, network blip, etc.)
     ↓
Each restarting controller does full LIST of all resources
     ↓
Heavy concurrent reads against all 3 etcd instances
     ↓
NVMe saturates (single consumer NVMe can't handle 3-way contention)
     ↓
Every process blocked on IO
     ↓
Probes fail (they're HTTP to apiserver, which is blocked)
     ↓
More controllers restart → MORE LIST storms → feedback
     ↓
Self-sustaining cascade
```

qemu-ga connection:
- qemu-ga CPU spike → VM briefly unresponsive
- kubelet marks pods unhealthy
- Pods restart → restart = full LIST
- Cascade trigger

qemu-ga is INDIRECT trigger via pod restart causing request spam.
Not direct via CPU starvation.

